<%#
  The bar that appears when rows are selected, naming the count and offering what can be done to
  them. Carbon's `TableBatchActions`, and the shape Gmail, GitHub, Jira and Material all use.

  Locals: `actions` is required -- an array of {label:, path:, icon:}.

  **It replaces the filter row rather than being inserted above it**, which is the whole point.
  Three of the four systems above put batch actions *in* the toolbar, and the reason is layout: an
  inserted row moves the table and a replaced one does not. The first version was a rounded,
  `brand-50` box wedged against the card's edges with no gap around it, and it pushed everything
  below it down 68px on every tick -- reported as "the blue background and no padding".

  There is no idle state and no hint. Nothing in Carbon, Gmail, GitHub or Material tells you that a
  checkbox can be ticked; the checkbox says that. When nothing is selected this is simply not there
  and the filter row is.

  **Replacing the filter row is also the right behaviour, not only the tidy one.** Filtering with a
  selection live is ambiguous -- does the selection survive rows leaving the page? -- and Carbon
  answers it the same way, by putting the filters out of reach until you are done. Cancel is one
  click away.

  The outer `mb-4` and the inner row's `mb-3` and `min-h-9` are the filter form's own, so the two
  occupy the same height to the pixel: measured, the table head stays at y=228 whether the toolbar
  or the bar is showing.
%>
<div class="mb-4" hidden data-table-selection-target="bar">
  <div class="mb-3 flex min-h-9 flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <%#
      `brand-50`, the same indigo the app already uses for a current tab, a focus ring and a
      selected row -- it means "this is what you are on". Carbon uses an inverse surface here; this
      app has no other one, so it would be the loudest thing on any page it appeared on.
    %>
    <p class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg bg-brand-50 px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-800"
       aria-live="polite"
       data-table-selection-target="count">0 selected</p>

    <% actions.each do |action| %>
      <%#
        An ordinary link. The chosen ids are appended to its query string by the controller, so a
        batch action is a navigation like any other -- no fetch, no form, and it opens in a new tab
        if that is what the reader asks for.
      %>
      <%= link_to action[:path],
            class: "#{essentials_button_classes(variant: :secondary)} aria-disabled:pointer-events-none aria-disabled:opacity-60",
            data: {table_selection_target: "action"} do %>
        <i class="<%= action[:icon] %>" aria-hidden="true"></i><%= action[:label] %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <button type="button"
            class="<%= essentials_button_classes(variant: :ghost) %> ms-auto"
            data-action="table-selection#clear">Cancel</button>
  </div>
</div>
